<p>Shoes are made on a last: a standardised mould that is widest at the ball of the foot and then tapers to a point. Your foot is the opposite shape. It is widest at the ends of your toes.</p>
<p>So every pair you have worn has been squeezing the widest part of your foot into the narrowest part of the shoe — then lifting the heel so your weight tips forward onto it. Do that for thirty years and you get exactly what most people over fifty are living with: toes pushed inward, pressure concentrated where the joint bulges out, and a foot that has slowly forgotten how to spread and grip.</p>
<p><a href="${GF_LINK}" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" target="_blank"><strong>Grounded Footwear</strong></a> is built the other way round. <strong>Widest at the toes. Flat from heel to forefoot. Thin enough to bend.</strong> That is the entire mechanism, and it is why owners describe noticing the difference in days rather than months.</p>

<h3>The sizing risk — and why it costs you nothing here</h3>
<p>Sizing is the number one reason barefoot shoes get returned. A foot-shaped toe box fits differently from anything you own, and a lot of people need a different size than they first order. On Amazon that means paying return postage and starting again.</p>
